1. kubectl describe node <node-name>
-> give information about the node

2. kubectl get --raw "/api/v1/nodes/<node-name>/proxy/metrics"
   kubectl get --raw "/api/v1/nodes/<node-name>/proxy/stats/summary"
-> hitting some api and asking kubelet of that node to get the info

3. kubectl get node -o wide
-> to get info about the node.

4. kubectl get clusterroles system:kubelet-api-admin -o yaml
-> to see the configuration of the resource

5. To check particular resource in all namespaces
-> kubectl get deployments -A

6. information about any type of resource
-> kubectl describe <resourcetype> <resourcename>





****************************************
Trouble-shooting
****************************************
1. Find the faulty pod
kubectl get pods -A

2. Now to find why a particular pod is failing
kubectl describe pod <pod-name>

3. To see the logs of containers inside pod.
-> kubectl logs <pod-name> -c <container-name>